  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1231 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nThe upper trough which plagued our region with persistent cloud\ncover, light showers, and cool temperatures over the weekend \nhas pushed off to our east, now positioned from New England \nsouth across the Appalachians into AL/GA. The southern part of \nthe trough gets cutoff and may slowly churn slightly \nnorthwestward Tues-Wed, but any convection associated with it \nlikely remains southeast of our cwa.\n\nNorthern stream energy will dive southeast across the Midwest on \nThursday, which allows a cold front to sink south closer to our \narea. Scattered convection is expected to develop and push into \nportions of our Thursday evening/overnight. 0-6km shear may increase \nto 25 kts or so along with SBCAPE of at least 2000 j/kg, which may \nlead to at least a lower end severe threat. If later timing pans out \nthough, convection may be in a weakening state as it pushes \nsoutheast into our area.\n\nAdditional chances for convection are possible Friday into Saturday \nwith mid level disturbances moving across the Ohio Valley. However, \nshear looks weaker (less than 20 kts) during this period, so should \nstruggle to get anything organized.\n\nTemperatures trend warmer mid to late week, but should primarily \nremain below 90 through Saturday. Trends over the past 48 hours have \nslowed down on the onset of our next heat potential, holding off \nuntil Sunday or even early next week before heat index readings may \nexceed 100 again. LREF does suggest a 50-60% chance at reaching 100+ \nheat index readings by next Tues/Wed. Current NBM dewpoints are back \ninto the mid 70s Sunday into mid next week, with the 75th percentile \nsuggesting 78-79 readings potentially returning. We may be on the \nouter periphery of the ridge next week which may allow convection to \nseep into parts of the area and tamp down the heat potential some. \nHowever, for now the best chance at MCS activity is north of us \nearly to mid next week.",
